Here's a Java program that calculates the diameter of a binary tree using its nodes and edges:

```
public class BinaryTreeDiameter {

  // Node class for binary tree
  class Node {
    int data;
    Node left, right;

    public Node(int data) {
      this.data = data;
      left = right = null;
    }
  }

  Node root;

  public BinaryTreeDiameter() {
    root = null;
  }

  // Recursive function to calculate height of a node
  int height(Node node) {
    if (node == null)
      return 0;

    int leftHeight = height(node.left);
    int rightHeight = height(node.right);

    return 1 + Math.max(leftHeight, rightHeight);
  }

  // Function to calculate diameter of binary tree
  int diameter() {
    return diameter(root);
  }

  // Recursive function to calculate diameter of a node
  int diameter(Node node) {
    if (node == null)
      return 0;

    int leftHeight = height(node.left);
    int rightHeight = height(node.right);

    int leftDiameter = diameter(node.left);
    int rightDiameter = diameter(node.right);

    return Math.max(leftHeight + rightHeight + 1,
                    Math.max(leftDiameter, rightDiameter));
  }

  public static void main(String[] args) {
    BinaryTreeDiameter tree = new BinaryTreeDiameter();

    // Creating nodes
    tree.root = tree.new Node(1);
    tree.root.left = tree.new Node(2);
    tree.root.right = tree.new Node(3);
    tree.root.left.left = tree.new Node(4);
    tree.root.left.right = tree.new Node(5);

    // Calculate diameter of binary tree
    int diameter = tree.diameter();
    System.out.println("Diameter of binary tree: " + diameter);
  }
}
```

The program uses two helper functions, `height()` to calculate the height of a node, and `diameter()` to recursively calculate the diameter of a node by considering the height of its left and right subtrees and the diameters of its left and right subtrees. The `main()` function creates a binary tree and calls `diameter()` to calculate its diameter.

Here is an example implementation of a hash table in Rust that uses open addressing:

```rust
use std::hash::{Hash, Hasher};

const DEFAULT_CAPACITY: usize = 16;

struct Entry<K, V> {
    key: K,
    value: V,
}

struct HashTable<K, V> {
    entries: Vec<Option<Entry<K, V>>>,
    size: usize,
}

impl<K: Hash + Eq, V> HashTable<K, V> {
    fn new() -> Self {
        Self {
            entries: vec![None; DEFAULT_CAPACITY],
            size: 0,
        }
    }

    fn put(&mut self, key: K, value: V) {
        if self.size >= self.entries.len() / 2 {
            self.resize();
        }

        let mut I = self.index(&key);
        while let Some(ref entry) = self.entries[i] {
            if entry.key == key {
                // Key already exists, update value
                self.entries[i] = Some(Entry { key, value });
                return;
            }

            I = (i + 1) % self.entries.len();
        }

        // Key does not exist, add new entry
        self.entries[i] = Some(Entry { key, value });
        self.size += 1;
    }

    fn get(&self, key: &K) -> Option<&V> {
        let mut I = self.index(key);
        while let Some(ref entry) = self.entries[i] {
            if entry.key == *key {
                return Some(&entry.value);
            }

            I = (i + 1) % self.entries.len();
        }

        None
    }

    fn remove(&mut self, key: &K) -> Option<V> {
        let mut I = self.index(key);
        while let Some(ref entry) = self.entries[i] {
            if entry.key == *key {
                let entry = self.entries[i].take();
                if entry.is_some() {
                    self.size -= 1;
                }
                return entry.map(|e| e.value);
            }

            I = (i + 1) % self.entries.len();
        }

        None
    }

    fn index(&self, key: &K) -> usize {
        let mut hasher = std::collections::hash_map::DefaultHasher::new();
        key.hash(&mut hasher);
        hasher.finish() as usize % self.entries.len()
    }

    fn resize(&mut self) {
        let mut new_entries = vec![None; self.entries.len() * 2];
        for entry in self.entries.iter_mut().filter(|e| e.is_some()) {
            let idx = self.index(&entry.as_ref().unwrap().key);
            new_entries[idx] = entry.take();
        }
        self.entries = new_entries;
    }
}
```

This implementation uses a vector to store entries, and rehashes on a resize. We use an `Option<Entry<K, V>>` so that entries can be removed without leaving gaps in the vector. 

To test the implementation, we can create a new hash table and add, remove, and get entries.

```rust
let mut table = HashTable::new();
table.put("one", 1);
table.put("two", 2);

assert_eq!(table.get(&"one"), Some(&1));
assert_eq!(table.remove(&"two"), Some(2));
assert_eq!(table.get(&"two"), None);
```

